# Strapi: Overview

**Strapi** open-source Headless CMS for developers that makes API creation easy. It supports:

- Creating API accessable models and components


---

# Content Types Builder

The  Content-type Builder allows the creation and management of content-types, which can be:

- Collection types: content-types that can manage several entries.
- Single types: content-types that can only manage one entry.
- Components: content structure that can be used in multiple collection types and single types. Although they are technically not proper content-types because they cannot exist independently, components are also created and managed through the Content-type Builder, in the same way as collection and single types.

## Overview

Strapi content types are organized in a specific folder structure under `strapi/src/api/`. Each content type consists of four main components:

1. **Schema Definition** (`schema.json`)
2. **Controller** (`controller.ts`)
3. **Routes** (`routes.ts`)
4. **Services** (`services.ts`)

## File Structure Pattern

```
strapi/src/api/
├── [content-type-name]/
│   ├── content-types/
│   │   └── [content-type-name]/
│   │       └── schema.json          # Defines structure & attributes
│   ├── controllers/
│   │   └── [content-type-name].ts   # HTTP request handlers
│   ├── routes/
│   │   └── [content-type-name].ts   # API endpoint definitions
│   └── services/
│       └── [content-type-name].ts   # Business logic
```

## Content Type Categories

### Collection Types
- **Purpose**: Manage multiple entries (many records)
- **Examples**: `article`, `artwork`, `category`, `author`
- **Schema**: `"kind": "collectionType"`
- **Use Cases**: Blog posts, products, users, events

### Single Types
- **Purpose**: Manage only one entry (single record)
- **Examples**: `about`, `global`
- **Schema**: `"kind": "singleType"`
- **Use Cases**: Site settings, about page, contact info

## File Components Explained

### 1. Schema Definition (`schema.json`)

The schema file defines the content type structure, attributes, and relationships.

**Key Properties:**
- `kind`: `"collectionType"` or `"singleType"`
- `collectionName`: Database table name
- `info`: Metadata (singular/plural names, display name, description)
- `options`: Configuration (draft/publish, etc.)
- `attributes`: Field definitions

**Example - Collection Type:**
```json
{
  "kind": "collectionType",
  "collectionName": "articles",
  "info": {
    "singularName": "article",
    "pluralName": "articles",
    "displayName": "Article",
    "description": "Create your blog content"
  },
  "options": {
    "draftAndPublish": true
  },
  "attributes": {
    "title": { "type": "string" },
    "description": { "type": "text", "maxLength": 80 },
    "slug": { "type": "uid", "targetField": "title" },
    "author": {
      "type": "relation",
      "relation": "manyToOne",
      "target": "api::author.author",
      "inversedBy": "articles"
    }
  }
}
```

**Example - Single Type:**
```json
{
  "kind": "singleType",
  "collectionName": "globals",
  "info": {
    "singularName": "global",
    "pluralName": "globals",
    "displayName": "Global",
    "description": "Define global settings"
  },
  "options": {
    "draftAndPublish": false
  },
  "attributes": {
    "siteName": { "type": "string", "required": true },
    "siteDescription": { "type": "text", "required": true }
  }
}
```

### 2. Controller (`controller.ts`)

Controllers handle HTTP requests and responses for the content type.

**Standard Pattern:**
```typescript
import { factories } from '@strapi/strapi';

export default factories.createCoreController('api::[content-type].[content-type]');
```

**Example:**
```typescript
// strapi/src/api/article/controllers/article.ts
import { factories } from '@strapi/strapi';

export default factories.createCoreController('api::article.article');
```

### 3. Routes (`routes.ts`)

Routes define the API endpoints for the content type.

**Standard Pattern:**
```typescript
import { factories } from '@strapi/strapi';

export default factories.createCoreRouter('api::[content-type].[content-type]');
```

**Example:**
```typescript
// strapi/src/api/article/routes/article.ts
import { factories } from '@strapi/strapi';

export default factories.createCoreRouter('api::article.article');
```

### 4. Services (`services.ts`)

Services contain the business logic for the content type.

**Standard Pattern:**
```typescript
import { factories } from '@strapi/strapi';

export default factories.createCoreService('api::[content-type].[content-type]');
```

**Example:**
```typescript
// strapi/src/api/article/services/article.ts
import { factories } from '@strapi/strapi';

export default factories.createCoreService('api::article.article');
```

## Common Attribute Types

### Basic Types
- `string`: Text input
- `text`: Textarea
- `richtext`: Rich text editor
- `email`: Email input
- `password`: Password input
- `number`: Number input
- `integer`: Integer input
- `biginteger`: Big integer
- `float`: Float number
- `decimal`: Decimal number
- `date`: Date picker
- `time`: Time picker
- `datetime`: Date and time picker
- `boolean`: Toggle/checkbox
- `enumeration`: Select dropdown
- `json`: JSON input

### Advanced Types
- `uid`: Unique identifier (often for slugs)
- `media`: File/image upload
- `relation`: Relationships between content types
- `component`: Reusable component
- `dynamiczone`: Dynamic zone with multiple components

### Relation Types
- `oneToOne`: One-to-one relationship
- `oneToMany`: One-to-many relationship
- `manyToOne`: Many-to-one relationship
- `manyToMany`: Many-to-many relationship

## Common Patterns

### 1. Creating a New Content Type

To create a new content type called `product`:

1. Create folder: `strapi/src/api/product/`
2. Create schema: `strapi/src/api/product/content-types/product/schema.json`
3. Create controller: `strapi/src/api/product/controllers/product.ts`
4. Create routes: `strapi/src/api/product/routes/product.ts`
5. Create services: `strapi/src/api/product/services/product.ts`

### 2. Relationships Between Content Types

**One-to-Many (Article belongs to Author):**
```json
// In article schema
"author": {
  "type": "relation",
  "relation": "manyToOne",
  "target": "api::author.author",
  "inversedBy": "articles"
}

// In author schema
"articles": {
  "type": "relation",
  "relation": "oneToMany",
  "target": "api::article.article",
  "mappedBy": "author"
}
```

### 3. Media Fields
```json
"cover": {
  "type": "media",
  "multiple": false,
  "required": false,
  "allowedTypes": ["images", "files", "videos"]
}
```

### 4. Dynamic Zones
```json
"blocks": {
  "type": "dynamiczone",
  "components": ["shared.media", "shared.quote", "shared.rich-text", "shared.slider"]
}
```

## API Endpoints Generated

### Collection Types
- `GET /api/[content-types]` - List all entries
- `GET /api/[content-types]/:id` - Get single entry
- `POST /api/[content-types]` - Create new entry
- `PUT /api/[content-types]/:id` - Update entry
- `DELETE /api/[content-types]/:id` - Delete entry

### Single Types
- `GET /api/[content-type]` - Get the single entry
- `PUT /api/[content-type]` - Update the single entry

## Best Practices

1. **Naming Convention**: Use lowercase, singular names for content types
2. **Schema Design**: Define clear relationships and appropriate field types
3. **Validation**: Set required fields and constraints in the schema
4. **Documentation**: Add meaningful descriptions to content types and fields
5. **Draft/Publish**: Use `draftAndPublish: true` for content that needs review
6. **Internationalization**: Use `i18n` plugin for multi-language content

## Example: Complete Content Type

Here's a complete example of a `product` content type:

**Schema** (`strapi/src/api/product/content-types/product/schema.json`):
```json
{
  "kind": "collectionType",
  "collectionName": "products",
  "info": {
    "singularName": "product",
    "pluralName": "products",
    "displayName": "Product",
    "description": "E-commerce products"
  },
  "options": {
    "draftAndPublish": true
  },
  "attributes": {
    "name": {
      "type": "string",
      "required": true
    },
    "description": {
      "type": "text"
    },
    "price": {
      "type": "decimal",
      "required": true
    },
    "slug": {
      "type": "uid",
      "targetField": "name"
    },
    "image": {
      "type": "media",
      "multiple": false,
      "allowedTypes": ["images"]
    },
    "category": {
      "type": "relation",
      "relation": "manyToOne",
      "target": "api::category.category",
      "inversedBy": "products"
    },
    "featured": {
      "type": "boolean",
      "default": false
    }
  }
}
```

This guide provides the foundation for understanding and working with Strapi content types. Each content type follows this consistent structure, making it predictable and easy to work with programmatically.

## Overview

Strapi components are reusable content structures that can be used across multiple content types. They help maintain consistency and reduce duplication by allowing you to define a structure once and use it in multiple places.

Components are stored in `strapi/src/components/` and organized by category folders.

## File Structure Pattern

```
strapi/src/components/
├── [category]/
│   ├── [component-name].json    # Component definition
│   ├── [component-name].json    # Another component
│   └── ...
├── [another-category]/
│   ├── [component-name].json
│   └── ...
```

**Example from your codebase:**
```
strapi/src/components/
├── shared/
│   ├── media.json
│   ├── quote.json
│   ├── rich-text.json
│   ├── seo.json
│   └── slider.json
```

## Component Structure

Each component is defined in a JSON file with the following structure:

```json
{
  "collectionName": "components_[category]_[component_name]",
  "info": {
    "displayName": "Component Display Name",
    "icon": "icon-name",
    "description": "Optional description"
  },
  "options": {},
  "attributes": {
    // Field definitions - same as content types
  }
}
```

## Real Examples from Your Codebase

### 1. Media Component (`shared/media.json`)
```json
{
  "collectionName": "components_shared_media",
  "info": {
    "displayName": "Media",
    "icon": "file-video"
  },
  "options": {},
  "attributes": {
    "file": {
      "allowedTypes": ["images", "files", "videos"],
      "type": "media",
      "multiple": false
    }
  }
}
```

### 2. Quote Component (`shared/quote.json`)
```json
{
  "collectionName": "components_shared_quotes",
  "info": {
    "displayName": "Quote",
    "icon": "indent"
  },
  "options": {},
  "attributes": {
    "title": {
      "type": "string"
    },
    "body": {
      "type": "text"
    }
  }
}
```

### 3. Rich Text Component (`shared/rich-text.json`)
